1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"claim-and-boundary","number":"12","title":"Attach each limitation to the result.","mode":"Detached caveats","scale":"slide","dek":"A closing slide should say which inference each limitation qualifies.","before":[{"id":"carbon-result","kind":"image","title":"GDP response","image":"/originals/stock-p25.png","sourcePage":"25","alt":"GDP response to a $40 carbon-tax increase, with 67% and 95% bands over years 0–6."},{"kind":"image","title":"Closing discussion · PDF p. 40","image":"/originals/stock-p40.png","alt":"Discussion caveats: spillovers to comparison countries; endogenous tax adoption and rate changes; interaction with the EU emissions-trading system; external validity.","id":"carbon-discussion","sourcePage":"40"}],"after":[{"kind":"carbon-response","title":"Estimated GDP effects remain close to zero.","label":"A $40 INCREASE IN THE CARBON TAX","note":"67% and 95% confidence bands · EU+ · Restricted local projections · Four lags","alt":"Rebuilt cumulative GDP-level response over years 0–6. Both 67% and 95% confidence bands include zero at every horizon.","revises":["carbon-result"]},{"kind":"claim","title":"The GDP response depends on the comparison and policy setting.","label":"CARBON TAXES AND GDP","items":[{"title":"Causal comparison","text":"Could endogenous tax changes or spillovers to comparison countries bias this response?"},{"title":"Policy setting","text":"These carbon taxes coexist with the EU emissions-trading system."},{"title":"Transfer","text":"Would a different tax design or another country produce the same response?"}],"note":"The confidence bands include both gains and losses in GDP.","revises":["carbon-discussion","carbon-result"]}],"diagnosis":"The closing slide lists valid limitations after the results, but leaves listeners to connect broad terms such as endogeneity and external validity to the estimate. Pairing each concern with the inference it limits makes the closing argument easier to retain.","changes":["Restate the result with its uncertainty.","Translate each caveat into a question about that result.","Keep the original response plot available with its full specification."],"preserved":"All four source concerns: spillovers, endogenous tax changes, interaction with emissions trading, and external validity. The original GDP-level plot and both confidence bands are retained.","deferred":"Other outcomes and specifications remain in the original deck. A near-zero estimate does not establish that the true effect is exactly zero.","practice":"Write “What this establishes” and “What it leaves open” in equally plain language. Check both with your advisor.","principle":"Be bigger than your paper","sourceType":"public","context":"FRBSF seminar. Results occupy PDF pp. 21–38 and are summarized on p. 39; p. 40 is the closing caveat slide. This local repair links its concerns to the cumulative GDP-level response on p. 25.","beforeCaption":"","afterCaption":"The GDP response, followed by its interpretation.","source":{"authors":"Gilbert E. Metcalf, James H. Stock","title":"The Macroeconomic Impact of Europe’s Carbon Taxes","date":"16 July 2020","url":"https://www.frbsf.org/economic-research/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/Stock-FRBSF-Seminar-071620.pdf","pages":"25, 40"},"values":["The GDP-level response is replotted from the chart on PDF p. 25. Coordinates were digitized from its raster plot and are approximate.","The plot retains horizons 0–6, both 67% and 95% bands, and the zero line. The vertical axis is tightened to ±3 percentage points so the bands can be read.","The experiment is a $40 carbon-tax increase in real 2018 US dollars, weighted by coverage share. Sample EU+; restricted local projections; parallel-path assumption; four lags.","Approximate point estimates at years 0–6: 0.04, 0.13, 0.31, 0.29, −0.04, 0.08, 0.08 percentage points."],"topic":"Climate & macro","point":"The estimate and the questions that limit its interpretation appear together. “Close to zero” remains a statement about an uncertain estimate, not proof of no effect."}}]
